import sys
T = {
"T1": ("Every security and compatibility result is reported as a difference between two arms run on the same host and the same platform release: a confined arm, in which the integration runs inside the framework, and an unconfined arm, in which the same integration runs as Home Assistant runs it today.",
       "Every security and compatibility result is the difference between a confined and an unconfined arm, run on the same host and the same platform release."),
"T2": ("The first runs of `watchman`, `adaptive_lighting`, `powercalc` and `scheduler` showed only that setup completed and that entities crossed the boundary, so a later retest gave each a stimulus and the value its own code implies, in both arms, and 42 of its 45 predictions held. Two of the three that failed concerned `scheduler`'s switch, which ends a single run as `triggered` rather than `off` in both arms, so the prediction misread the integration rather than the boundary. The third was a defect in the framework. Confined, `scheduler`'s command reached the platform without its target, because the bridge dropped a target given as a list, and the decision record named no device for any service call, whatever its form. Both were fixed after the cut-off, with a test that failed before the fix, and the table reports the corrected result. `watchman` shows a difference between the arms that is correct rather than a fault:",
       "The first runs of `watchman`, `adaptive_lighting`, `powercalc` and `scheduler` showed only that setup completed, so a later retest gave each a stimulus and the value its own code implies, in both arms, and 42 of its 45 predictions held. Two of the misses misread `scheduler`'s switch, which ends a run as `triggered` in both arms. The third was a defect in the framework: confined, `scheduler`'s command reached the platform without its target, because the bridge dropped a target given as a list, and no decision record named the device of a service call. Both parts were fixed after the cut-off, each with a test that failed first, and the table reports the corrected result. `watchman` differs between the arms correctly rather than by fault:"),
"T3": ("The platform's integration quality rules recommend that an integration obtain its HTTP client through the shared helper `async_get_clientsession` [22]. In the platform release under test, that helper constructs its DNS resolver through Zeroconf, which joins a multicast group on start-up. A freshly created network namespace provides a loopback interface without multicast enabled, so the join failed and `nordpool`, the first real integration run against a real API, could not construct an HTTP client at all. The earlier test subjects had opened a bare client session and so bypassed the code path that ordinary integrations use. Until this was found, the egress design did not work for the cloud-facing integrations it was built to serve, while every test suite passed. Enabling multicast on the sandbox's loopback interface, with a multicast route to that interface alone, corrected the fault without granting any route out of the sandbox.",
       "The platform's quality rules recommend that an integration obtain its HTTP client through a shared helper [22], and in the release under test that helper joins a multicast group on start-up. A new network namespace has no multicast on its loopback interface, so `nordpool`, the first real integration run against a real API, could not construct an HTTP client at all. The test subjects had opened a bare client session and never reached that code path, so every suite passed while the egress design failed the cloud-facing integrations it was built to serve. Enabling multicast on the sandbox's loopback interface alone corrected the fault without granting any route out."),
"T4": ("An earlier estimate that left out the audit write put the headroom at about 11,000 times; the figure quoted here includes it. ", ""),
"T5": ("A change in Home Assistant reaches a confined integration in 0.50 ms at the median, and the median has stayed under a millisecond in every run on record. It does, however, move between runs: the verification passes gave medians of 0.71 to 0.93 ms, six runs on one evening gave 0.50 to 0.66 ms (finding F46), and the final rerun gave 0.50 to 0.52 ms across its five passes, 30% below the 0.71 ms previously stated and outside the ±20% band fixed for it (finding F62).",
       "A change in Home Assistant reaches a confined integration in 0.50 ms at the median, and in under a millisecond in every run on record. The median nevertheless moves between runs, from 0.50 to 0.93 ms (finding F46), and the final rerun's figure fell 30% below the 0.71 ms previously stated, outside the ±20% band fixed for it (finding F62)."),
"T6": ("Twelve were rated Major, four between Minor and Major, 32 Minor, and four were notes or requests to re-measure. One remains open (F18), one is retained as a residual risk (F11) and one is stated as a limitation (F53); the rest are resolved. A functional retest after the cut-off added three more, all resolved: the framework defect in Section 12.2 that left a confined command without its target (F54, Major), and two faults in the evaluation's own unconfined arm (F55, F56, Minor). Checking Section 9 against the code added four more (F57–F60), the most serious that a sandbox could start without the user's approval (F57, Major), and the final rerun two (F61, F62, Minor), so Appendix F lists 62.",
       "One remains open (F18), one is retained as a residual risk (F11) and one is stated as a limitation (F53); the rest are resolved. Nine more followed the cut-off, all resolved: a functional retest found the framework defect that left a confined command without its target (F54, Major) and two faults in the evaluation's own unconfined arm (F55, F56); checking Section 9 against the code found four (F57–F60), the most serious that a sandbox could start without the user's approval (F57, Major); and the final rerun found two (F61, F62). Appendix F lists all 62 with their ratings."),
"T7": ("The requirement that integrations run unmodified was reported as verified mechanically before any comparison of source trees existed; when the comparison was run, 75 tree comparisons found no change, but the report had been wrong for two months. And the scoring of the security results against both threat registers, promised in the evaluation's design, was missing until a read-through supplied it.",
       "The requirement that integrations run unmodified was reported as verified two months before any comparison of source trees existed, although the 75 comparisons later found no change, and the promised scoring of the security results against both threat registers was missing until a read-through supplied it."),
"T8": ("so every regression run since the refusal-flood work had recorded a pass that could not have been a failure.",
       "so every regression run had recorded a pass that could not have been a failure."),
"T8b": (" A second test integration that does not stop now exercises it.", ""),
}
src, dst = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]
s = open(src).read(); saved = {}
for k,(a,b) in T.items():
    assert s.count(a)==1, k
    s = s.replace(a,b); saved[k] = len(a.split())-len(b.split())
open(dst,'w').write(s)
print(saved, "total", sum(saved.values()))
